[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
The failure is not in OSPF or routing; it’s the DNS daemon on the dns_server that is down. From host_1_1_1_1, ICMP to 10.200.0.2 succeeds (low RTT, 0% loss), proving the campus path is healthy. However, DNS lookups to 10.200.0.2 return “connection refused.” On the dns_server, there is no listener on port 53 (netstat shows no :53 sockets), and systemd reports bind9.service is inactive (dead). With /etc/resolv.conf on host_1_1_1_1 pointing to 10.200.0.2 and nsswitch set to “files dns,” the resolver sends queries to 10.200.0.2:53; because named is not running, the kernel returns ICMP port unreachable, which dig/nslookup report as “connection refused.” This directly explains the symptom while ruling out any path or OSPF issue.

Localization: dns_server, named/bind9 process; network interface is fine (it responds to ping). Root cause: the bind9 service on dns_server is stopped/disabled, so nothing is listening on 10.200.0.2:53.

Corrective fix: restart and enable the DNS service on dns_server. Commands: systemctl start bind9; systemctl enable bind9. Then verify with netstat -tuln | grep :53 and test dig web0.local @10.200.0.2. No router or OSPF restart is required; only the dns_server’s bind9 service needs to be brought up. If persistence is needed, also confirm /etc/bind config contains zones for web0.local/web1.local and that named listens on all interfaces.
=== END ===